WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.762
So what does Conditional
Access actually evaluate?

2
00:00:02.762 --> 00:00:06.696
First, signals: who the user
is and what roles they hold,

3
00:00:06.696 --> 00:00:11.075
their device, the application,
the location, and sign-in risk.

4
00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:15.100
Conditions decide which policies
apply to this particular request.

5
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.891
Then controls: block, or
allow with requirements,

6
00:00:18.891 --> 00:00:23.250
like MFA, a compliant device
or an app protection policy.

7
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.875
And session controls, such as
how often to sign in again.

8
00:00:26.875 --> 00:00:29.575
Three operating rules
run through the library.

9
00:00:29.575 --> 00:00:32.503
Every policy runs in
report-only mode first,

10
00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:35.838
so you see its impact before
it enforces anything.

11
00:00:35.838 --> 00:00:39.081
Policies target personas,
like admins, staff,

12
00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:42.675
guests and workload
identities, not individuals.

13
00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:45.938
And deleted policies can be
recovered for thirty days.

14
00:00:45.938 --> 00:00:51.913
One recent change is worth knowing: the
approved client app grant went read-only

15
00:00:51.913 --> 00:00:57.220
on June 30, 2026, so mobile access
now relies on app protection policies.
